 label(\"$C$\",vertices[1],N); label(\"$B$\",vertices[2],NW); label(\"$A$\",vertices[3],SW);\nlabel(\"$F$\",vertices[4],S); label(\"$E$\",vertices[5],SE);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"fb98ec97ffbc1747ab50d81f4454b2a4067298d8aa503ecf44a5971975db53f0"},"seconds":39.74808216094971,"seed":926374796249506268,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem step-by-step, we start by identifying the lengths and areas of the components of the star figure, which consists of two equilateral triangles and a central hexagon, all made up of smaller equilateral triangles.\n\nGiven that \\(\\overline{AC}\\) is 3 units long, we note that \\(\\overline{AC}\\) is a side of one of the larger equilateral triangles in the star. This means that the side length of these larger triangles is 3 units.\n\nThe area of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nFor the larger equilateral triangles, with \\(s = 3\\), the area of one triangle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 3^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 9 = \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\]\nSince there are two such triangles, the combined area of the two larger triangles is:\n\\[\n2 \\times \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{18\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to consider the central hexagon. The hexagon can be divided into 6 smaller equilateral triangles, each with a side length equal to half the side length of the larger triangles (since the hexagon fits perfectly inside the star and shares sides with the smaller triangles that form the star). Therefore, the side length of each of these smaller triangles is 1.5 units.\n\nThe area of one of these smaller equilateral triangles is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times (1.5)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 2.25 = \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{16}\n\\]\nSince there are 6 such smaller triangles in the hexagon, the area of the hexagon is:\n\\[\n6 \\times \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{16} = \\frac{54\\sqrt{3}}{16} = \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{8}\n\\]\n\nThe total area of the star is the sum of the areas of the two larger triangles and the central hexagon:\n\\[\n\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{8} = \\frac{36\\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{8} = \\frac{63\\sqrt{3}}{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the entire star is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{63\\sqrt{3}}{8}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{63\\sqrt{3}}{8}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
